      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "CONTEXT",
          "content": "Obesity in men is associated with low serum testosterone and both are associated with several diseases and increased mortality."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "Examine the direction and causality of the relationship between body mass index (BMI) and serum testosterone."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Bi-directional Mendelian randomization (MR) analysis on prospective cohorts."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "Five cohorts from Denmark, Germany and Sweden (Inter99, SHIP, SHIP Trend, GOOD and MrOS Sweden)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PARTICIPANTS",
          "content": "7446 Caucasian men, genotyped for 97 BMI-associated SNPs and three testosterone-associated SNPs."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES",
          "content": "BMI and serum testosterone adjusted for age, smoking, time of blood sampling and site."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "1 SD genetically instrumented increase in BMI was associated with a 0.25 SD decrease in serum testosterone (IV ratio: -0.25, 95% CI: -0.42--0.09, p = 2.8*10-3). For a body weight reduction altering the BMI from 30 to 25 kg/m2, the effect would equal a 13% increase in serum testosterone. No association was seen for genetically instrumented testosterone with BMI, a finding that was confirmed using large-scale data from the GIANT consortium (n = 104349)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Our results suggest that there is a causal effect of BMI on serum testosterone in men. Population level interventions to reduce BMI are expected to increase serum testosterone in men."
        }
      ]},
